{#
  The guest view (FSD §8.4, Mockup 11-wand-gaeste).

  "Name + Grußzeile aus HA, gesetzt im Panel, dargestellt in großer
  Schreibschrift auf einem Hintergrundbild." [Festlegung C13]

  Everything that could be decided has been decided already, in
  ``guest_layout.py``: which face, at which size, broken into which lines, and
  how tall the band behind them is. This file only draws it. That split is the
  lesson from the first recipe image — CSS re-wrapping what Python had budgeted
  for is how text runs off a canvas nobody is watching.

  The band is the first of the three remedies FSD §8.4 names against the dither
  raster of the photo (band → quiet picture area → outline). Grey 200, flat and
  several hundred pixels tall: the measured failure of grey was a 2 px rule
  breaking up into dots, which a large area does not do. It is dropped when
  there is no picture — on flat white it would be a grey stripe for nothing, and
  grey on white is the one pairing that carries a visible raster right where the
  text sits.

  Dependency-free apart from the two file:// URLs it is given (the picture and
  the font file). Both are already on local disk when this renders.
#}<!doctype html>
<html lang="{{ language }}">
  <head>
    <meta charset="utf-8" />
    <title>ePaperEngine</title>
    <style>
      @font-face {
        font-family: "epaperengine-script";
        src: url("{{ plan.font_url }}");
      }
      html,
      body {
        margin: 0;
        padding: 0;
        width: 2560px;
        height: 1440px;
        overflow: hidden;
        /* White rather than black: a guest page whose picture failed to load
           should read as a plain greeting card, not as a fault. The photo view
           uses black for the opposite reason — there, a missing picture *is*
           the failure. */
        background: #fff;
        color: #000;
      }
      .stage {
        position: relative;
        width: 2560px;
        height: 1440px;
      }
      .stage img {
        display: block;
        width: 2560px;
        height: 1440px;
        object-fit: cover;
      }
      .band {
        position: absolute;
        left: 0;
        width: 2560px;
        background: rgb({{ band_grey }}, {{ band_grey }}, {{ band_grey }});
      }
      .text {
        position: absolute;
        left: 0;
        width: 2560px;
        box-sizing: border-box;
        padding: 0 160px;
        text-align: center;
        /* The family is named *and* declared through @font-face above; the two
           are the same file. "cursive" is the last resort and would be visible
           at a glance, which is the point of naming it rather than sans-serif. */
        font-family: "{{ plan.font_family }}", "epaperengine-script", cursive;
        font-weight: {{ plan.font_weight }};
      }
      .line {
        white-space: nowrap;
      }
      .name .line {
        font-size: {{ plan.name.font_px }}px;
        line-height: {{ line_height }};
      }
      .greeting .line {
        font-size: {{ plan.greeting.font_px }}px;
        line-height: {{ line_height }};
      }
      /* Nothing typed yet. Not an error — the section exists from the first
         start — so it says so quietly in the one font that is always there. */
      .empty {
        font-family: "DejaVu Sans", sans-serif;
        font-size: 56px;
        color: #555;
      }
    </style>
  </head>
  <body>
    <div class="stage">
      {% if plan.background_url %}<img src="{{ plan.background_url }}" alt="" />{% endif %}
      {% if plan.band %}
      <div class="band" style="top: {{ plan.band_top }}px; height: {{ plan.band_height }}px"></div>
      {% endif %}
      <div class="text" style="top: {{ plan.band_top + band_pad_y }}px">
        {% if plan.name.lines or plan.greeting.lines %}
        <div class="name">
          {% for line in plan.name.lines %}<div class="line">{{ line }}</div>{% endfor %}
        </div>
        {% if plan.greeting.lines %}
        {# The gap only exists between two blocks. Hard-coding it as a margin
           would add 48 px that ``guest_layout`` did not put into the band when
           only one of the two fields is filled. #}
        <div class="greeting" style="margin-top: {{ block_gap if plan.name.lines else 0 }}px">
          {% for line in plan.greeting.lines %}<div class="line">{{ line }}</div>{% endfor %}
        </div>
        {% endif %}
        {% else %}
        <div class="empty">{{ t("guests.empty") }}</div>
        {% endif %}
      </div>
    </div>
  </body>
</html>
